Human–LLM Internal Loop (HIL)

A Causal Architecture for Persistent Latent Social Simulation

Ouadi Maakoul — with AI collaborators

Theoretical PhD Draft — Version 0.3


Abstract

Human conversation involves persistent internal dynamics that continue during pauses, listening, and silent intervals. Large language models (LLMs) demonstrate latent computation and internal reasoning (Quiet-STaR; Coconut), but latent computation alone does not establish a persistent, socially structured internal state capable of tracking an interlocutor across time. We propose the Human–LLM Internal Loop (HIL) as a testable architectural framework. HIL does not address consciousness or phenomenology. It posits that interactive social cognition depends on a set of functionally separable but dynamically coupled latent variables—most critically, an explicit representation of the Other agent O_t that participates causally in the agent's policy.

The central hypothesis of v0.3 is refined:

H1 (Non-redundant sufficiency). Under matched computational budgets, persistent latent recursion alone is insufficient for persistent social-state simulation; explicit causal coupling between Self and Other representations within a persistent recurrent state is a non-redundant sufficient architectural property.

We propose a four-architecture comparison under matched training FLOPs, inference latency, memory, and depth: (1) standard autoregressive LLM, (2) latent-reasoning architecture without inter-turn persistence (Coconut-style), (3) persistent recurrent latent architecture without Self/Other factorization (Huginn-style, Mamba/RMT-style), and (4) HIL with persistent latent state and explicit Self/Other factorization. The principal test uses distributed alignment search (DAS) and counterfactual interchange interventions on O_t, rather than post-hoc SAE clamping:

\text{do}(O_t = \tilde O_t) \quad\text{implemented as}\quad \mathrm{Interchange}(O_t^{\text{source}} \to O_t^{\text{base}})

with path patching to isolate the O_t \to a_t pathway. If controlled interventions systematically modify subsequent behavior in the predicted direction—while equivalent computational budgets without explicit O_t factorization fail to reproduce the effect—this provides evidence that the representation of the Other is causally effective rather than epiphenomenal.

The proposed framework shifts the research question from "Does an LLM think like a human?" to "Which architectural mechanisms are sufficient for persistent, causally effective social simulation during interaction?"

