4. Operationalizing O_t

This is the critical methodological section. v0.2 used SAE feature clamping; v0.3 replaces this with causal abstraction + DAS + path patching.

4.1 Why predictive information is not enough

The v0.2 criterion I(Y_B; O_t \mid C_{\text{text}}) > 0 is necessary but not sufficient. Many features may encode the interlocutor without being used by the policy. We strengthen to:

\boxed{\ I(Y_B; O_t \mid C_{\text{text}}, W_t, S_t, R_t) > 0\ }

and require that O_t be a minimal sufficient statistic for Y_B given the rest of the state.

4.2 Why SAE clamping is not a do-intervention

SAE clamping: (i) creates off-manifold states, (ii) may modify multiple variables simultaneously, (iii) does not respect the causal graph. We replace it with interchange interventions on counterfactual runs [16, 18].

4.3 Concrete protocol for O_t

Step 1 — Architectural encoder. Train LoRA g_O on frozen Llama-3-8B with supervision from true interlocutor hidden states Y_B (from the NegotiationToM BDI labels or PDDL-Mind transition semantics).

Step 2 — SAE discovery. Train Gemma-Scope-style TopK SAEs on layers 20–24 (16k dictionary, L_0 = 32). Find top-k features predictive of Y_B.

Step 3 — DAS rotation. Learn orthogonal R isolating the O_t subspace:

\min_{R} \ \mathbb{E}_{(x_{\text{base}}, x_{\text{src}})}\!\left[ \mathcal{L}_{\text{task}}\!\left( \mathrm{Dec}\!\left(\mathrm{Interchange}_R(\mathrm{Enc}(x_{\text{base}}), \mathrm{Enc}(x_{\text{src}}))\right) \right) \right]

so that the low-level model conforms to the high-level causal model with O_t as a variable.

Step 4 — Counterfactual interchange. Source = cooperative interlocutor; base = competitive. Patch O_t features from source into base at matched positions. Measure:

\Delta_a = D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left( \pi(a_t \mid \mathrm{do}(O_t = \tilde O_t)) \,\|\, \pi(a_t \mid \mathrm{do}(O_t = O_t^*)) \right)

on a forced-choice policy classifier (response-strategy classifier), not open-ended generation.

Step 5 — Path patching. Isolate the O_t \to a_t edge from O_t \to W_t \to a_t etc., using path patching [18]. This resolves the multiple-mediator problem.

4.4 Architectural factorization vs. post-hoc identification

To claim HIL is architecturally factorized rather than merely interpretable, we require:

1. Separate recurrent modules f_S and f_O with gated cross-attention.
2. A contrastive MI objective:

\mathcal{L} = I(O_t; Y_B) - \lambda_1 I(O_t; S_t) - \lambda_2 I(S_t; Y_B),

with \lambda_1, \lambda_2 > 0, used as a regularizer (MI estimation is unstable and may collapse).

3. A DAS check on the Z-only model: apply DAS to the Huginn-style R_t-only model to ask whether an O_t-equivalent subspace exists via rotation. If DAS succeeds, the factorization claim is weakened; if it fails, the architectural claim is strengthened.

