5. Causal Graph and Intervention Semantics

5.1 Unrolled SCM

The macro-graph u_t \to W_t \to O_t \to P_t \to C_t \to E_t \to a_t is cyclic once re-entry is included. We therefore define the SCM on the unrolled internal graph:

X^{(k+1)}_v = f_v\!\left( \mathrm{pa}(X^{(k)}_v), u_t, \epsilon^{(k)}_v \right), \quad k = 0, \dots, K-1,

for each variable v \in \{W, S, O, R\}, with edges allowed per step as specified in §3.3–3.5.

5.2 Effect definitions

· Total effect:

\mathrm{TE} = \mathbb{E}[a_t \mid \mathrm{do}(O_t = \tilde O_t)] - \mathbb{E}[a_t \mid \mathrm{do}(O_t = O_t^*)].

· Path-specific effects via path patching rather than NDE/NIE. With non-linearities and latent re-entry, NDE/NIE are not identifiable without sequential ignorability [20, 21]. Path patching gives a computable decomposition:

\mathrm{TE} = \sum_{p \in \Pi(O_t \to a_t)} \mathrm{PSE}(p) + \mathrm{residual},

where \Pi is the set of directed paths in the unrolled graph.

5.3 Stochastic policies

For stochastic policies, measure distributional shift:

\Delta_a = D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left( \pi(a_t \mid \mathrm{do}(O_t = \tilde O_t)) \,\|\, \pi(a_t \mid \mathrm{do}(O_t = O_t^*)) \right).

