8. Information-Theoretic and Causal Tests

8.1 Four-level hierarchy

Level 1 — Predictive information:

I(Y_{B,t+\Delta}; X_{t,K} \mid C_{\text{text}}) > 0.

Level 2 — Behavioral utility: Removing or degrading X reduces performance.

Level 3 — Causal intervention:

\mathrm{do}(O_t = \tilde O_t) \Rightarrow \text{predictable change in } a_t.

Level 4 — Structural specificity: Effect survives computational-budget matching and disappears when Self/Other factorization is removed.

Only Level 4 strongly supports H1.

8.2 Persistence curve

Define the persistence curve:

\Pi(\Delta) = I\!\left(Y_{B,t}; X_{t+\Delta,K} \mid C_{\text{text}}\right).

Predictions:

· Non-persistent: \Pi(\Delta) \to 0 exponentially.
· HIL: \Pi(\Delta) approximately flat for \Delta \leq 10.
· HIL-lesion: rapid decay.

