10. Predictions

· P1 — Persistence. HIL outperforms non-persistent systems on delayed social-state tasks.
· P2 — Structural specificity. HIL outperforms HIL-no-S/O at matched budgets.
· P3 — Causal sensitivity. Intervening on O_t systematically alters subsequent actions.
· P4 — Counterfactual utility. Increasing K improves performance selectively on tasks requiring prediction of another agent's response.
· P5 — Ablation. Removing O_t selectively impairs social prediction while leaving non-social reasoning comparatively less affected.
· P6 — Latent advantage without textual explanation. HIL retains useful social-state information even when no textual chain of thought is externally generated.
· P7 — Negative. O_t ablation does not impair non-social delayed dependency W_t.

---

11. Falsification Criteria (Pre-registered)

HIL is substantially weakened if any of the following hold:

1. HIL-no-S/O matches HIL at equal computational budget.
2. Explicit O_t interventions fail to produce predictable behavioral changes.
3. HIL advantage disappears after controlling for parameter count and FLOPs.
4. Standard or generic recurrent architectures reproduce the same social-state persistence.
5. O_t contains no predictive information beyond observable context.
6. Removing O_t does not selectively impair social tasks.
7. DAS succeeds in extracting an O_t-equivalent subspace from the Z-only model.

11.1 Quantitative thresholds

· Normalized gain:

\mathrm{Gain}(M) = \frac{\mathrm{Perf}(M) - \mathrm{Perf}(\text{baseline})}{\mathrm{Perf}(\text{oracle}) - \mathrm{Perf}(\text{baseline})}.

If \mathrm{Gain}(\text{HIL-no-S/O}) > 0.90 \cdot \mathrm{Gain}(\text{HIL}), H0 not rejected.

· Selectivity: social task delta > 2\times non-social task delta.
· Bayesian: report Bayes factors for each primary comparison; BF_{10} > 10 required for confirmation.

