15. Conclusion

The central thesis of HIL v0.3 is:

Persistent latent recursion alone is insufficient under matched compute; explicit causal Self/Other coupling within a persistent recurrent state is a non-redundant sufficient architectural property for persistent social-state simulation.

HIL does not claim to create consciousness. It proposes a narrower, experimentally attackable proposition:

\boxed{\ \text{Persistent latent recursion} + \text{Self/Other structure} + \text{causal re-entry} \xrightarrow{\text{matched compute}} \text{enhanced social-state simulation}\ }

By operationalizing O_t as a DAS-identified, interchange-intervenable subspace, and by running path-patched do-interventions with matched compute across four architectures and three controls, HIL becomes a testable experimental program rather than a philosophical speculation.

---

Core Research Question

What minimal recurrent architecture is sufficient to maintain a causally effective internal model of another agent across time, including periods without observable output?

Not: "Does the machine think?"

But: "What must a machine be architecturally capable of doing for internal social simulation to become a persistent dynamical process?"


