Appendix D: DAS and Path Patching Implementation Protocol

D.1 DAS Rotation Search

Learn orthogonal rotation R isolating the O_t subspace:

\min_{R} \ \mathbb{E}_{(x_{\text{base}}, x_{\text{src}})}\!\left[ \mathcal{L}_{\text{task}}\!\left( \mathrm{Dec}\!\left(\mathrm{Interchange}_R(\mathrm{Enc}(x_{\text{base}}), \mathrm{Enc}(x_{\text{src}}))\right) \right) \right]

Reference: Wu et al., Interpretability at Scale: Identifying Causal Mechanisms in Alpaca, arXiv:2305.08809.

D.2 SAE Training Protocol

Parameter Value
Dictionary size 16k
L_0 32
Layers 20–24
Architecture TopK SAE
Model Llama-3-8B

Reference: Lieberum et al., Gemma Scope: Open Sparse Autoencoders, arXiv:2408.05147.

D.3 Counterfactual Interchange Protocol

Step 1 — Architectural encoder: Train LoRA g_O on frozen Llama-3-8B with supervision from true interlocutor hidden states Y_B (from NegotiationToM BDI labels).

Step 2 — SAE discovery: Train Gemma-Scope-style TopK SAEs on layers 20–24. Find top-k features predictive of Y_B.

Step 3 — DAS rotation: Learn orthogonal R isolating the O_t subspace.

Step 4 — Counterfactual interchange: Source = cooperative interlocutor; base = competitive. Patch O_t features from source into base at matched positions.

Step 5 — Path patching: Isolate the O_t \to a_t edge from O_t \to W_t \to a_t etc.

D.4 DAS Check on Z-Only Model

Apply DAS to the Huginn-style R_t-only model. If DAS succeeds in extracting an O_t-equivalent subspace via rotation, the factorization claim is weakened. If it fails, the architectural claim is strengthened.

